1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,759
Your Spring monolith ships. Teams grow.
Deploys queue behind each other. One noisy
feature takes the whole process down.
Someone says the word: microservices. That
word is not a trophy.

2
00:00:12,879 --> 00:00:26,439
Microservices are a scalability and
organization choice — and distributed
failure is the price of admission. If you
cannot name the organizational or scale
problem you are solving, you are not ready
to pay that price.

3
00:00:26,559 --> 00:00:36,479
Start with boundaries by business
capability. Orders are not "the database
layer" and "the web layer" split into two
repos that still share one schema and must
deploy together.

4
00:00:36,599 --> 00:00:51,079
That split is a distributed monolith:
network latency with none of the
independence. Service A owns orders;
Service B owns inventory. They communicate
through APIs or events — not by reaching
into each other's tables.

5
00:00:51,199 --> 00:01:01,879
Look at this java for a moment.
Independent deployability is the test. Can
the orders team release on Tuesday without
a coordinated inventory release, as long
as contracts hold?

6
00:01:02,059 --> 00:01:09,539
Two services that must change a shared
library and a shared database migration in
lockstep are one system wearing two
costumes.

7
00:01:09,659 --> 00:01:20,579
Contracts — versioned APIs, consumer-
driven tests, or event schemas — make
independence real. Data ownership follows
the same honesty: each service owns its
persistence.

8
00:01:20,699 --> 00:01:30,659
The shared "reporting" schema everyone
writes to couples you silently; serve
reporting via events, careful read
replicas, or a dedicated analytics
pipeline. The network is unreliable.

9
00:01:30,779 --> 00:01:39,699
Calls time out, retry, duplicate, arrive
reordered. What was a method call in a
monolith becomes a failure mode you must
design for.

10
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:50,419
Partial failure is the everyday case:
inventory succeeded, billing timed out,
the user clicked again. Idempotency and
correlation ids are not optional extras.

11
00:01:50,539 --> 00:02:00,099
Ignoring observability in that world is
negligence — you cannot debug a graph of
services with stdout on one laptop.
Modular monolith first often wins.

12
00:02:00,219 --> 00:02:08,419
Keep module boundaries inside one
deployable until the boundaries hurt for
real — separate release cadence, separate
scaling, separate failure isolation.

13
00:02:08,539 --> 00:02:14,979
That often means packages or Gradle
modules with enforced boundaries, not a
wish and a wiki diagram.

14
00:02:15,099 --> 00:02:24,819
Splitting by technical layers only —
"controller service" calling "logic
service" calling "data service" — creates
chatty services with none of the autonomy.

15
00:02:24,939 --> 00:02:30,859
Prefer vertical slices of business
capability that can answer meaningful
requests with minimal synchronous fan-out.

16
00:02:30,979 --> 00:02:40,819
Organizational scaling is a valid reason
to split — Conway's law is not a joke —
but only when teams already struggle to
ship inside one codebase with clear
modules.

17
00:02:40,939 --> 00:02:53,620
If one team owns everything, microservices
mainly add operational tax. When not
microservices? Small team, simple domain,
early product — a modular monolith may win
on focus and operability.

18
00:02:53,740 --> 00:03:04,740
Say that in interviews without
embarrassment. Then say what would change
your mind: independent scaling of a
hotspot, team autonomy blocked by a single
release train, or a clear bounded context
ready to isolate.

19
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:16,940
Return to the monolith morning. Deploys
queue because every feature shares one
release train. That organizational
friction can justify extraction even
before traffic demands it — if the domain
boundary is real.

20
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:26,979
Extract the inventory context behind an
API, give it its own database, keep orders
talking through a client with timeouts.
Measure whether deploy frequency actually
rises.

21
00:03:27,099 --> 00:03:41,060
If it does not, you paid the network tax
for nothing. One closing caution:
microservices multiply JVM processes, each
with its own heap, GC, metaspace, and
startup profile.

22
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:52,620
Everything from Episodes Fifty-Six through
Sixty-Five becomes a fleet concern. If you
cannot operate one JVM well, operating
thirty will not teach you by magic — it
will page you in parallel.

23
00:03:52,740 --> 00:04:05,980
Microservices without visibility and
without timeouts become a slower monolith
with worse nights. The next pressure is
observability and resilience — how you see
the system and how you survive dependency
failure.
